window.docContents[238].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics","header":"8.5. Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Abstract-Minimization":{"contents":"A weakly lower-semicontinuous real functional on a complete real Hilbert\nspace attains a global minimum if the sublevel through some supplied\n is norm bounded:Then there is  such that  for every .\nThis is the compactness-plus-liminf core of the direct method.\n\nThe sublevel through  is weakly closed by lower semicontinuity, weakly\ncompact by boundedness, and nonempty because it contains . The\nfunctional therefore attains a minimum there. That minimum is at most\n, while every point outside the sublevel has larger energy, so the\nconstrained minimum is global.\n\nThe bounded-sublevel hypothesis must be checked at a specific point ;\ncoercivity removes that bookkeeping.\n\nIf  is weakly lower semicontinuous and coercive,then  has a global minimizer.\n\nCoercivity makes the sublevel through the origin norm bounded. Apply the\nbounded-sublevel direct-method theorem to that nonempty sublevel.\n\nBoth existence theorems assume lower semicontinuity for the weak topology, a\nhypothesis that is awkward to verify directly because weakly convergent nets\nneed not converge in norm. Convexity is the standard way to obtain it from the\nnorm topology.\n\nEvery convex real functional on a real normed space that is lower semicontinuous in norm\nis lower semicontinuous for the weak topology:This is the functional-analytic step behind Evans's integral-functional\nargument.\n\nFor every real , the sublevel  is convex because\n is convex and norm closed because  is norm lower semicontinuous. A\nnorm-closed convex set is weakly closed by Hahn–Banach separation. Thus every\nsublevel is weakly closed, which is equivalent to lower semicontinuity of\n in the weak topology.\n\nCombined with the coercive existence theorem, this criterion removes the weak\ntopology from the hypotheses. Completeness of  is still required, since\nthe weak compactness theorem uses it.\n\nA convex functional on a complete real Hilbert space has a global minimizer\nif it is lower semicontinuous in norm and coercive:\n\nConvexity and norm lower semicontinuity give weak lower semicontinuity. The\ncoercive direct method theorem then supplies a minimizer.\n\nConvexity alone leaves room for a whole convex set of minimizers, as a constant\nfunctional shows. Strict convexity excludes this: the midpoint of two distinct\nminimizers would have strictly smaller energy.\n\nStrict convexity upgrades the direct-method conclusion to a unique global\nminimizer:\n\nStrict convexity supplies convexity, so the argument with closed convex sublevel sets\ngives weak lower semicontinuity. The weak direct method gives existence, and\nstrict convexity identifies any two global minimizers.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.5. Abstract Minimization","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Abstract-Minimization"}});